pve-wedge-watchdog for Debian / Proxmox VE
==========================================

The service is installed DISABLED and the shipped config has dry_run = true.

 1. Pick a watchdog device that watchdog-mux does not hold (README.md,
    "Which watchdog device"); set `watchdog =` in /etc/pve-wedge-watchdog.conf.
 2. Run in dry-run for a few days: systemctl enable --now pve-wedge-watchdog
    and watch `journalctl -u pve-wedge-watchdog` for false verdicts.
 3. Test arming in a throwaway VM first (testing.md), then set dry_run = false
    and restart.

`systemctl stop` disarms cleanly (magic close) unless the daemon has already
decided the host is wedged, in which case the reboot proceeds.

Passthrough memory guard
------------------------

Unlike the watchdog, pve-passthrough-guard-sync.{service,timer} are ENABLED on
install. They attach /usr/share/pve-wedge-watchdog/passthrough-memory-guard.sh
(copied to /var/lib/vz/snippets/) as the hookscript of every VM on this node
with hostpci* or hugepages: that has no hookscript yet. The guard only refuses
VM starts whose pinned RAM does not fit MemAvailable minus a margin. VMs with
a different hookscript are left alone (warning in the journal). Bypass: list
the vmid in PVE_GUARD_SKIP in /etc/default/pve-passthrough-guard, or touch
/etc/pve-passthrough-guard.d/<vmid>.skip. See README.md.
